    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. One of my main concerns is the \"bad faith\" aspect—basically, making sure they don't try to pull any fast ones or hide certain costs until the last minute. How do we write the contract to account for those kinds of hidden risks or untrustworthy behavior from the vendor?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To combat bad faith, you should include explicit anti-defraud provisions that prohibit the supplier from concealing costs or creating artificial backorders. You may also want to add a detailed audit right clause that lets you inspect their records freely without penalty to verify their claims and inventory levels. Additionally, defining \"material\" faults and tied to breach clauses can encourage them to be upfront early rather than hiding issues until the last minute.",
      "source": "target",
